#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# matroid_rank

#% text
# `matroid_rank(M), (M, S)`: the rank of the matroid or of the subset S

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A matroid is `linear_matroid(A)` (the columns of a rational matrix), `graphic_matroid(edges)` (the edges of a graph), `uniform_matroid(r, m)` or `matroid(bases)` (bases as lists of elements 1, ..., m); its elements are numbered 1, ..., m, at most 22.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
matroid_rank(linear_matroid([[1, 0, 1, 1], [0, 1, 1, 2]]))
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
matroid_rank(linear_matroid([[1, 0, 1, 1], [0, 1, 1, 2]]), [3, 4])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
matroid_rank(uniform_matroid(2, 5), [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `matroid_bases`, `matroid_circuits`, `tutte_polynomial`
